A||||18||William Federle||2.5 Starter replacemt||||1||
Z||000000||William Federle||01-05-2000||02:54 PM||||Can someone tell me if there is any special trick to getting the starter off of a 1984 2.5 engine? The Haynes and Chilton books don't say to remove any other parts except for the catyltic convertor heat shield and I don't see any heat shield on my catyltic convertor. It just looks like there isn't enough room to get it out. I'm going to try tonight because I have to replace the starter-solenoid.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Scott2M4||01-05-2000||05:14 PM||||What's your starter doing that makes you think you need to replace the soleniod?  I ask because my starter is behaving strangely and I'm trying to figure out why.<P>Every once in awhile (not every time and in various climates (warm/cold/wet/dry)), the starter will whine like it's running but the motor doesn't turn over.  Someone said the bendix(?) was not engaging the flywheel and that it could be caused by many different things (bad starter, bad or dirty connections, low voltage).<P>Does your problem sound similar?||||reg||5
Z||000002||fierospeeder||01-05-2000||07:36 PM||||first disconnect the battery, lift the car up, put jacks.  Underneath disconnect the two wires unless its three, i forgot, that is on the starter.  There are two small sheet metal guards for the flywheel which are held on by three machine screws.  After that the starter is held by three bolts, one on the passenger side, and two that are vertical on the drivers side, and a shim is also attached on that side.  Watch so the starter doesn't drop on you, and slide it out towards the passenger side and pull it towards the front of the car and angle it up and it should come down.  I didn't need to touch any of the exhaust compenents.<P>GMs are known to have the solenoids to go bad because of the heat, and ford has a separate solenoid in the engine bay.  You can buy a heat shield for your starter.||||reg||1
Z||000003||William Federle||01-05-2000||08:08 PM||||The reason I have to replace the solenoid is that I was tightening the nut up on the pos. battery wire on it and the stud failed - it's just anchored in plastic. I found the wire loose when I was troubleshooting a situation in which the car would miss and the tach would simultaneously jump and the shift light would come on when slowing down to 55 from highway speeds (on a snowy and rainy day). Also the car was getting harder to start. A new solenoid is available from Advance Auto for $9.99 + tax so maybe I'll be back on the road with this thing before the end of the week - unless something breaks while I'm gettig the starter out. <P>Thanks for the info on getting it out Fierospeeder.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 01-05-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||DRH||01-05-2000||08:16 PM||||William,<P>I have never tried this but... <P>You MIGHT be able to replace the solenoid with the starter in place. It is held on by 2 screws and an electrical connection to the starter motor. Once these are out you need to rotate it a few degrees to unlock a tab from the starter. What I'm not sure of is if you can get to both of the screws that hold it to the starter with the starter in place.<P>Might be worth a look...<P>David.||||reg||1
Z||000005||William Federle||01-05-2000||10:36 PM||||Thanks - maybe I'll try to snake a mirror up there to have a look and see if I can get a wrench on those screws.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Rodrv6||01-06-2000||07:23 AM||||It is possible to change the solonoid without removing the starter.  I've done it a couple of times on my 84. You can do it all from the top, but you will have to work mostly by feel since it's hard to get hands, tools, and eyes into the same spot.||||reg||10
Z||000007||Patrick||01-06-2000||03:51 PM||||<BR>I'm a little puzzled guys. Why is there all this effort being put into changing the solenoid without taking the starter off? I changed the starter on my '87 2.5L 5speed and it was pretty straight forward. The worse part of course, was lying under the car in very cramped conditions, holding the starter in place with one hand while trying to start the first mounting bolt with the other. True, it wouldn't be any fun to drop the starter on your head, but at least everything was plainly visible. Are other years or models more difficult to work on than mine? I would think that all the 4-bangers would be very similar.||||reg||5
Z||000008||William Federle||01-06-2000||08:01 PM||||I took a good look at the solenoid from under the car and tried to get a socket wrench on both screws - the inside screw is very difficult to get the wrench on. So I desided to take the starter out. I found the heat shield they talk about - it's on the passenger side - two screws and it's out. The starter came out in the space where the shield was. I tried to take the solenoid off with the starter out. The hex heads of the screws were so rusty that they rounded off when I tried to break them loose with a socket wrench. I don't want it to be so screwed up that Advanced Auto won't take it in lieu of a core charge. It looks like I have to get a new starter - $44.95 + $25.00 core charge (which I hope to avoid) Well - at least there's one more maintenance item on a Fiero that I'll be able to take care of.||||reg||1
Z||000009||DRH||01-06-2000||08:17 PM||||If the bolts are that bad, the starter probably is on the tail end of it's life anyway. They should take it as a core unless it is the wrong one or has been severely abused (which you are avoiding). Plus the new starter should come with a solenoid so you should recoup $10 there. Quite a bit of piece of mind for $35!||||reg||1
Z||000010||Patrick||01-06-2000||08:29 PM||||<BR>Hmmmm.... I don't remember having to remove any heat shield to get at my starter. Is there supposed to be one that is necessarily in the way when removing the starter? If so, am I cooking my starter?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000011||William Federle||01-06-2000||09:31 PM||||To tell you the truth, it may be some sort of splash shield. It's secured by two sheet metal screws and its located on the passenger side. With it removed there is just enough room to get the starter through the bend in the exhaust pipe there. I called it a heat shield because I remembered the haynes book saying to remove the catylitic convertor heat shield. I don't think this piece of metal has anything to do with the cat though.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Shipbuilder||01-07-2000||07:41 PM||||I believe the heat shield is there to protect the coolant hose from the heat off the exhaust pipe||||reg||1
Z||000013||Patrick||01-07-2000||08:45 PM||||<BR>I guess I'm going to have to crawl under there and reacquaint myself with the underside of my Fiero. Oh well, it's time to change the oil again anyway. Which leads me to this question...<P>Is there any way to change the <B>oil filter</B> on the '84-'87 2.5L engine that doesn't result in filthy old oil running everywhere when the filter is removed? Has anyone tried puncturing the filter and draining the oil from it first?<P>Maybe I should have put this in a new thread, but heck, we're in the same general area already. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000014||William Federle||01-07-2000||10:24 PM||||I've begun cramming old rags or paper towels<BR>between the engine and that mount that's right below the oil filter. <P>Ive got my new starter. I took the solenoid off of it so that I could manually activate the pinion to see how it meshes with the flywheel gear. I don't see how I could see the mesh though since it would be on the other side of the little "bell housing". The old starter didn't have any shims. Is this an indication that I might not need any with this one?||||reg||1
Z||000015||Patrick||01-07-2000||10:58 PM||||<BR>Hmmmm..... I wouldn't assume anything. It's probably variations in the starter casings that result in the need for shims.||||reg||4
Z||000016||fierospeeder||01-08-2000||12:30 AM||||i think all the starters mounted this way need shims.  Pepboys carries them so does trak auto.  <BR>I didn't need to take my splash shield off either, i know the shield is right against the firewall.||||reg||1
Z||000017||William Federle||01-08-2000||11:50 AM||||Is there a typical thickness that you might have to shim a reman'd starter? I guess that sounds like an unanswerable question. I can't really see how I can get to the mesh to measure clearance. I can just barely see it. But I have been trying to get a feel for how tight it is by taking the solenoid off and working the engaging mechanism by hand with the starter bolted in place. You have to play with the flywheel to get the starter engaged and then it drags when you pull it out. Should there be absolutely no drag? I have 2 of the 3 .042" shims that came in a pack from advance auto, now. And it still drags slightly when I disengage it. Anybody done this? Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000018||William Federle||01-08-2000||04:15 PM||||I finally got the starter installed. With all three .042 shims in I could just see a little gap between the bottom of the extended gear and the flywheel gear. More than half of the tooth is meshed. (I think at least 3/4). It starts like a new car. This is probably due to the fact that the wires are securely fastened to the starter (which they weren't before). Thanks to everyone who replied.<p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 01-08-2000).]||||reg||1
